WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:05.240
There are plenty of shocking finales in Hollywood
history, but it takes a special kind of climax

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:07.940
to make someone leave the theater in a fit
of rage.

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:12.400
These movie endings all sparked major controversies,
sometimes for the silliest of reasons.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:13.460
Major spoilers ahead.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:18.560
When 10 Cloverfield Lane arrived in 2016,
people wondered if this sci-fi flick would

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:23.240
have any connection to the J.J. Abrams produced
monster movie Cloverfield that'd come out

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:24.499
eight years before.

00:00:24.499 --> 00:00:26.099
Was it a sequel or a prequel?

00:00:26.099 --> 00:00:27.749
Did it take place in the same universe?

00:00:27.749 --> 00:00:28.909
Would there be any aliens?

00:00:28.909 --> 00:00:33.420
As moviegoers quickly found out, there were
most definitely aliens, but not everyone was

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:35.980
happy when the evil ETs showed up on screen.

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:44.700
"Oh, come on."

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:49.840
The space worms first appear after our hero
Michelle escapes from an underground bunker.

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:54.390
For most of the movie, she's been held captive
by Howard, a murderous conspiracy theorist

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:56.660
who thinks aliens have taken over the world.

00:00:56.660 --> 00:01:00.560
And when Michelle finally makes her way to
the surface, she discovers Howard was right.

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:05.230
Seconds after defeating her warden, Michelle
is forced to fight against invaders from another

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:05.720
galaxy.

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:20.060
This fiery finale ticked off quite a few moviegoers,
as many thought the climax was overblown and

00:01:20.069 --> 00:01:21.069
out of place.

00:01:21.069 --> 00:01:25.119
Some said the movie "self-destructed" in the
last few minutes, while others argued Michelle

00:01:25.119 --> 00:01:29.549
didn't need to fight any extraterrestrials
as she'd already beaten Howard.

00:01:29.549 --> 00:01:33.189
On the other hand, other critics say the scene
is essential for Michelle's character arc.

00:01:33.189 --> 00:01:38.049
Now that she's seen the alien foe up close,
her decision to join the resistance has real

00:01:38.049 --> 00:01:39.049
meaning.

00:01:39.049 --> 00:01:43.109
She now knows from firsthand experience what
dangers lie ahead, so when she drives toward

00:01:43.109 --> 00:01:47.289
the invasion, we understand she's finally
overcome her fear, and she won't run away

00:01:47.289 --> 00:01:48.289
this time.

00:01:48.289 --> 00:01:51.729
But regardless of your opinion on the ending,
can we all agree that John Goodman was way

00:01:51.729 --> 00:01:53.120
scarier than any alien?

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:54.120
"I'm sorry."

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:57.880
"I accept your apology."

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:07.389
Directed by Nicholas Meyer, Star Trek II:
The Wrath of Khan features one of the saddest

00:02:07.389 --> 00:02:10.160
death scenes you'll ever see in a sci-fi movie.

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:14.700
After going toe-to-toe with Khan, the crew
of the USS Enterprise is in serious trouble.

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:19.250
There's a super weapon about to go off, and
Captain Kirk can't escape the blast in time

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:22.640
because the ship's warp drive has been seriously
damaged.

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:24.920
And that's when Spock decides to save the
day.

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:29.450
Putting the needs of the many ahead of the
needs of the few, Spock exposes himself to

00:02:29.450 --> 00:02:32.560
deadly radiation in hopes of repairing the
ship.

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:34.820
"No human can tolerate the radiation that's
in there."

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:40.520
"As you are so fond of observing, Doctor,
I am not human."

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:44.760
While his plan works out and the Enterprise
escapes intact, our pointy-eared hero dies

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:48.480
a tragic death, separated from Kirk by a pane
of glass.

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:52.021
It's a heartbreaking scene, and if you're
not getting a bit misty-eyed right now, it's

00:02:52.021 --> 00:02:53.840
probably because you're a Vulcan.

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:56.520
"How do you feel?

00:02:56.860 --> 00:02:58.760
How do you feel?"

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:03.650
"I do not understand the question."

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:07.750
Of course, fans back in 1982 weren't just
upset about Spock's demise.

00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:09.700
They were downright furious.

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:13.760
News of Spock's death leaked pretty early
during the filming of Wrath of Khan, resulting

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:15.830
in a major outcry from fans.

00:03:15.830 --> 00:03:20.600
According to executive producer Harve Bennett,
the filmmakers received "a hundred thousand

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:22.450
letters" pleading for Spock's life.

00:03:22.450 --> 00:03:26.450
One Trekkie took out a magazine ad demanding
that Paramount spare the Vulcan, and some

00:03:26.450 --> 00:03:31.380
fans were so upset that they started harassing
actor Leonard Nimoy, actually threatening

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:32.580
the man and his family.

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:36.630
Hoping to throw fans off, Meyer and company
added a scene early in the film in which Spock

00:03:36.630 --> 00:03:41.260
fakes his death during a training sequence,
and honestly, that probably only made it hurt

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:46.410
even more when the Vulcan finally gave his
trademark salute for the final time…well,

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:48.960
until Star Trek III: The Search for Spock
came out, anyway.

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:53.900
When The Devil Inside hit theaters in 2012,
there were multiple reports that audience

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:57.810
members began jeering and shouting in anger
after the credits started rolling.

00:03:57.810 --> 00:04:01.990
So what kind of ending would cause moviegoers
to unleash their inner demons?

00:04:01.990 --> 00:04:07.200
Well, The Devil Inside is a found footage
film about a killer named Maria who's possessed

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:08.300
by four demons.

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:12.760
Her daughter, Isabella, is making a documentary
about her murderous mom, and eventually, the

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:16.950
film culminates with an exorcism where the
demons decide to leave their host and inhabit

00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:17.950
our heroes.

00:04:17.950 --> 00:04:22.120
As a result, things take an unfortunate turn
as Isabella and her friend are speeding down

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:23.120
a busy highway.

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:27.620
Suddenly, her possessed buddy turns into oncoming
traffic, and the movie cuts to black.

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:37.340
And that's when a title card pops up, reading,
"The facts surrounding the Rossi case remain

00:04:37.340 --> 00:04:38.340
unresolved.

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:43.130
For more information about the ongoing investigation,
visit www.TheRossiFiles.com."

00:04:43.130 --> 00:04:48.720
Really, the site was just a bizarre bit of
viral marketing, but moviegoers thought they

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:51.780
would have to visit the site to find out how
the story ended.

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:56.430
That wasn't actually the case, but nevertheless,
people felt cheated, prompting a whole lot

00:04:56.430 --> 00:04:58.600
of outrage from furious horror fans.

00:04:59.140 --> 00:05:03.960
Based on the novel by Nicholas Sparks, Safe
Haven has a terrible approval rating on Rotten

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:07.130
Tomatoes, and a lot of that hatred has to
do with an ending that comes straight out

00:05:07.130 --> 00:05:08.690
of The Sixth Sense.

00:05:08.690 --> 00:05:13.030
Supposedly set in the real world, you know,
a place where ghosts don't exist, this 2013

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:15.750
romance focuses on a battered wife named Katie.

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:19.990
She's on the run after stabbing her husband,
and she eventually winds up in a new town

00:05:19.990 --> 00:05:23.870
where she befriends a local woman named Jo
and falls in love with a hunky widower named

00:05:23.870 --> 00:05:24.870
Alex.

00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:28.510
As you've probably guessed, Katie and Alex
wind up together, but moments before the credits

00:05:28.510 --> 00:05:33.520
roll, the couple discovers a stack of letters
written by Alex's dead wife, Carly.

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:38.330
They also find a photo of his old flame, and
surprise, she looks exactly like Jo.

00:05:38.330 --> 00:05:39.500
Yeah, that's right.

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:44.450
Katie's friend Jo is the ghost of Alex's wife,
returned from the afterlife to play matchmaker

00:05:44.450 --> 00:05:45.450
for her hubby.

00:05:45.450 --> 00:05:50.000
As twists go, this one is completely nuts,
especially considering there weren't any apparent

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:52.340
supernatural elements in the movie until the
end.

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:56.530
In fact, the finale was so bonkers that one
critic wrote that the twist "will make you

00:05:56.530 --> 00:05:59.680
want to punch the movie in the face."

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:02.400
"You bitch!

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:05.110
You bitch!"

00:06:07.830 --> 00:06:12.260
Directed by Seth Rogen and Evan Goldberg,
The Interview follows a talk show host and

00:06:12.260 --> 00:06:16.770
his producer as they visit North Korea to,
well, interview Kim Jong-un.

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:21.900
But before they reach Asia, they're recruited
by the CIA to assassinate the dictator.

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:26.550
Initially reluctant, they finally do the deed
when Kim decides to launch his nuclear weapons.

00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:31.150
As Kim sits in a helicopter, James Franco
and Seth Rogen blast him to kingdom come with

00:06:31.150 --> 00:06:35.620
a tank, and we watch in slow-mo as the bad
guy's face catches on fire.

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:38.300
Originally, Kim's death was way more violent.

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:42.120
His entire head exploded, sending "chunks
of skull" everywhere.

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:46.180
This did not sit well with the CEO of Sony,
who insisted that Rogen tone down the gore.

00:06:46.180 --> 00:06:49.430
But that was just the start of what would
become an international incident.

00:06:49.430 --> 00:06:52.870
The madness really began when North Korea
stated that releasing The Interview would

00:06:52.870 --> 00:06:54.530
be an "act of war."

00:06:54.530 --> 00:06:58.729
Things only got worse when a North Korean-backed
group called the Guardians of Peace hacked

00:06:58.729 --> 00:07:03.310
Sony, began releasing private emails, and
demanded the studio shut down the controversial

00:07:03.310 --> 00:07:04.520
comedy.

00:07:06.220 --> 00:07:09.400
"Oh no, it's happening."

00:07:09.410 --> 00:07:11.360
And this is where things got kind of scary.

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:14.960
The Guardians of Peace followed up their hacking
attack with terrorist threats.

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:20.169
As a result, five major theater chains decided
to drop The Interview, and Sony initially

00:07:20.169 --> 00:07:22.130
declined to release the movie online.

00:07:22.130 --> 00:07:26.110
Sure, the movie eventually did make its way
into theaters and streaming services, but

00:07:26.110 --> 00:07:29.570
it's kind of weird to think that decades from
now, film students will still be talking about

00:07:29.570 --> 00:07:32.970
how a ridiculous Seth Rogen movie sparked
an international incident.

00:07:32.970 --> 00:07:33.970
"They hate us!"

00:07:33.970 --> 00:07:36.120
"They hate us because they ain't us."

00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:37.900
"Kim!

00:07:38.060 --> 00:07:39.060
You got it buddy!"

00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:40.180
"Okay!

00:07:40.180 --> 00:07:41.180
Alleyoop!"

00:07:42.460 --> 00:07:46.980
Even though Do the Right Thing came out nearly
30 years ago, it's just as relevant today

00:07:46.990 --> 00:07:48.930
as it was in 1989.

00:07:48.930 --> 00:07:53.020
Set during a vicious summer heat wave in New
York City, this Spike Lee joint focuses on

00:07:53.020 --> 00:07:55.110
a small neighborhood divided by race.

00:07:55.110 --> 00:07:58.770
The film takes place over the course of a
single day, and we watch as an argument between

00:07:58.770 --> 00:08:02.550
a pizza shop owner and two customers escalates
into a violent showdown.

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:06.950
The madness starts when an NYPD officer kills
a young African American man.

00:08:06.950 --> 00:08:11.720
In response, the main character starts a riot
that destroys the aforementioned pizza parlor.

00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:16.440
"Hey!"

00:08:22.180 --> 00:08:26.760
It's a devastating climax, one that leaves
audiences shaken to the core but empathetic

00:08:26.770 --> 00:08:29.060
toward all the characters and where they're
coming from.

00:08:29.060 --> 00:08:31.270
Well, that's what was supposed to happen anyway.

00:08:31.270 --> 00:08:34.930
Unfortunately, a lot of people misunderstood
the movie and worried Do the Right Thing would

00:08:34.930 --> 00:08:36.820
incite real-life riots.

00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:38.039
One reviewer at the time wrote:

00:08:38.039 --> 00:08:43.070
"The end of this movie a shambles, and
if some audiences go wild, [Lee is] partly

00:08:43.070 --> 00:08:44.070
responsible."

00:08:44.070 --> 00:08:47.730
Other critics apparently felt the same, way
with one going to far as to say that the movie

00:08:47.730 --> 00:08:49.610
could have political ramifications.

00:08:49.610 --> 00:08:52.839
Naturally, this didn't sit well with Lee,
who told Rolling Stone:

00:08:52.839 --> 00:08:56.190
"I don't remember people saying people were
going to come out of theaters killing people

00:08:56.190 --> 00:08:58.210
after they watched Arnold Schwarzenegger films."

